 I came up with some new ideas to NetHack a while back, and I 
thought I would share them with you (and of course the dev team; 
I suppose they are reading this newsgroup). I want to hear your 
comments/criticism about what I have to offer here. In my opinion, 
the ideas presented below are very much in the spirit of NetHack. 
That cannot be said about all the new ideas presented on this news- 
group - I am referring mainly to the phone booth idea here. 
 
1) Mushrooms. A real dungeon must have some plant growth within, 
and mushrooms fit in the best. Mushrooms could grow everywhere, 
although they would be most common near water (especially the 
pools on the lower levels).  
 I had thought about ten kinds of mushrooms.  
 1) Common edible mushroom. No special effects, gives 40 nutrition 
points (or whatever they're called). (**) 
 2) Large edible mushroom. As above, but gives 100 nutrition points. (**)
 3) Noxious dungeon mushroom. 30 nutrition points. For characters 
with low constitution there should be a significant risk of losing 
hit points+strength+constitution, whereas for characters with very 
high constitution this risk should be relatively low (but existent). (**) 
 4) Deadly agaric. 50 nutrition points. Dangerous effects beginning 
in 50-100 turns (during this time you can stop the effect by drinking 
a potion of extra healing. The trick is that you have absolutely no 
way of knowing that you've been poisoned <see below>): confusion,  
loss of about half of hit points, loss of 1-4 points of strength 
and constitution (at this point you can only reverse the confusion, death 
and hit point effect by drinking a potion of extra healing. Of course 
you can bring back the lost stats by potions of gain ability). After 
50 more rounds: death. Characters with 15+ constitution might not 
die: constitution 15 (80% risk of dying), 16 (70%), 17 (50%), 18 (30%). 
 5) Enchanted agaric. 40 nutrition points. Random potion effect.  
Usually these mushrooms should be blessed when found. Very rare.  
 6) Hallusinogenic cap. 50 nutrition points. After 40-100 rounds, 
it should begin functioning as a potion of hallucination (with only 
100-200 turns of hallucination, though). (**) 
 7) Sleeping beauty. 30 nutrition points. After 100 turns, for the 
next 1000 turns there should be a 30% chance of falling asleep for 
10 turns every 75 turns (complicated sentence, huh?). Sleep resis- 
tance will prevent effects, whereas a potion of extra healing will 
not (but a unicorn horn will).  
 8) Magic mushroom. 200 nutrition points. Raises one stat by 1-2 
points permanently. Restores all hit points.  
 9) Healing agaric. 50 nutrition points. Functions as a potion of 
extra healing. (**) 
 10) Wizard's hat. 10 nutrition points. Raises intelligence and 
wisdom by 2-4 points. The rarest mushroom of all.  
 
 With the exception of enchanted agaric, magic mushroom and 
wizard's hat, you should be able to take 3 bites from every 
mushroom.  
 Since mushrooms are growing entities, this fact should also be 
considered. I was thinking that in order to make the mushroom 
grow and reproduce, you should drop a fresh corpse (fungi often 
grow on rotten/rotting things) to the square next to the shroom, 
and wait 200-250 turns in the vicinity for warmth-providing. After 
200-250 turns, another mushroom of the same kind should appear 
next to its "father". In order to prevent abuse of this feature, 
no more than one extra shroom should ever grow. You also must not 
pick up the shroom if you want to make it grow. Only the mushrooms 
marked with (**) could be grown like this. 
 Caveman will be able to identify all mushrooms and their effects. 
 
 
2). Slimes. It is only natural to suppose that some kind of slimes 
also exist in the dungeons. I am suggesting that they only appear 
in the corridors. Size could vary from 1 to 3 squares. Normally, 
slimes wouldn't do anything but in some cases they would be acidic 
and affect your boots (lower their value by 2-3 points. If you're 
not wearing boots, a loss of 5-10 hit points would occur; your legs 
should also become "hurt" for 50-100 turns, making you unable to 
kick), and sometimes what appears to be mere slime should turn out to 
be an ochre jelly when you step into it.  
 A dog/cat would not step into a dangerous slime (their instinct 
tells it just as they can tell a healthy piece of food from an 
unhealthy one).  
 A wand of fire/lighting should destroy a slime and show the true 
nature of an ochre jelly.  
 
3) Special monsters. The barlog, the nazgul and the named demons/ 
devils are way too easy to kill! Suppose that one of them makes 
3d6 points of damage. Turn this into 6d3 and make everything else 
about him harder, too.  

4) Dragons. Also much too easy to handle. In mythology, dragons
often (if not always) guard a vast treasure. How about making a
new kind of dragon, the Gold Dragon (and to hell with it being
lawful in D&D etc). Gold dragons would reside in rather large 
rooms, resting on piles of *treasure*. They should be next to
impossible to kill. Need I mention that they also should be
master wizards? No more than one Gold dragon should appear in
one game. No bones from Gold dragon levels. 

5) Soldiers, beehives, zoos and throne rooms. 
Usually (if not always) when I enter a room mentioned above,
all of the creatures inside are sleeping. There is nothing
particularly wrong with this, but it's ridiculous that none
of the creatures - with the exception of the one I'm fighting
at the moment - wake up from the noise! Especially soldiers
should always have one on guard, and he should blow alert
when necessary. 

